kkomkkomi RevenueCat claims function

This directory contains a modified copy of the HTTPS handler of the RevenueCat
Firebase extension "firestore-revenuecat-purchases", from the repository
https://github.com/RevenueCat/firestore-revenuecat-purchases at commit
8089f19e948b3326040425de158fa1233891d675 (version 0.2.0). That work is licensed
under the Apache License, Version 2.0, whose text is in LICENSE in this
directory.

The source files derived from it are src/index.ts, src/handler.ts,
src/payload.ts, and src/types.ts. Each one names the upstream files that it
comes from.

Modifications made for kkomkkomi in 2026:

- The handler is a Cloud Functions v2 HTTPS function in asia-northeast3 on the
  nodejs24 runtime, written as a function of its dependencies (Auth, secret,
  clock, logger) so that it can be tested without Firebase.
- The shared secret comes from the Secret Manager secret
  REVENUECAT_WEBHOOK_SECRET, read at request time. An empty secret is refused
  with status 500, and the JWT algorithm is fixed to HS256.
- The Firestore events collection, the Firestore customers collection and its
  document watermark, the Eventarc channel, the moment dependency, the
  X-EXTENSION-VERSION header, and the API version check are removed. The
  function writes only the revenueCatEntitlements and
  revenueCatEventTimestampMs custom claims.
- The custom claims are read and written through one injected Auth instance.
- A verified payload without an event, or without the customer entitlements of
  a user that it names, is refused with status 500, as the upstream handler
  failed on it, but before any write.
- The read of the watermark and the claim write of one user are serialized in
  a per-user queue inside the instance, and the function is limited to one
  instance, so that two overlapping events of one user cannot both pass the
  watermark check and let the older one write last. The upstream handler has
  no such serialization.
- A claim write that fails is logged and answered with status 200, as before;
  one that fails because the user does not exist is logged as a warning.
- Error responses carry a message only, without an error code or extension
  version.
- The tests were written for this repository; their token helper signs the
  payload claim in the same shape as the upstream test helper.
